WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.720
Wake the f**k off Americans?!
I'm here to talk some sense into you!

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Well, hold on a second, I'm Canadian.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.000
And when I say Americans,
I'm including Canadians in that, because...

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.940
Canadians, admit it,
we're really just the 51st state

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.160
We're like California with igloos.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.500
So, Canadians, Americans... same thing

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.940
Anyway, what I was saying?

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.620
Wake the f**k off Americans?!
I'm here to talk some sense into you

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.680
As a nation, we suck with languages.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.680
43% of all people around the world are bilingual.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.780
That's includes over 50% of the people in Europe.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.480
And in some countries like Indonesia and the Philippines, 
virtually everybody is bilingual.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.740
And it's very common that a lot of countries, 
for people to speak 3, 4 or even 5 languages.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.000
On a regular basis

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.540
But, in America, only 20% of the people are bilingual.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.660
And guess what...

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.760
Almost all of those people are  immigrants 
or the children of immigrants.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.280
Amongst homegrown Americans, very few are bilingual.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.340
And in the northern province of Canada,
the situation is not much different.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.920
Most of the people who are bilingual,
are immigrants or Francophone Canadians,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.140
french-speaking Canadians

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.800
Anglophone Canadians are generally monolingual.
Very few of them are bilingual.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.280
In Australia, the numbers are even worse.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.640
And in Europe, guess which country has 
the lower level of bilingualism?

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:19.520
Hint:

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.080
The UK and Ireland!

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.180
So the point I'm making is that Anglophones 
basically do NOT become bilingual.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.420
I don't want to say Anglophones are lazy, but...

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.760
Why the hell are we so damn lazy?!

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.000
Well, obviously, it's because of a lack of incentive

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.400
English is the international language

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.940
So, why should I put in all this time
and effort to speak another language

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.220
when other people will just speak 
english with me?

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.660
Isn't it kind of sort of arrogant to expect all the other 
people to put in all the effort to accommodate us?

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.480
Anybody who has traveled have problably seen Americans or other Anglophones

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.240
getting frustrated with the locals 
when they couldn't speak English.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.160
But, if those Anglophones had ever learned 
a second language for themselves,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.000
maybe they would be able to understand 
and empathize with that other person

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.960
and find the way to communicate with them

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.900
The truth is: English is NOT spoken 
by most people in the world,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.600
despite the fact that it's 
the de facto international language

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.760
Most people simple don't speak English, 
unless you stick to the main tourist traps.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.240
And who likes cheesy tourist traps?

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.460
Knowing another major language can greatly 
help you to communicate around the world

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.060
there's also no guarantee that English will be 
the international language for much longer.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.260
Not too long ago, the international language was French.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.140
And in the future, maybe, it will be Chinese or Spanish.
Who knows?

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.640
Well, problably, won't be Chinese, but, you never know.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:47.880
The number of Spanish-speakers is growing rapidly 
and a lot of Spanish-speaking countries are 
in the process of developing rapidly.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.180
So, Spanish could become much 
more important in the near future.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.940
But, even if Spanish never becomes
the de facto international language,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:00.080
there's no denying that knowing Spanish would be 
incredibly useful in a lot of places around the world.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.960
What do I care? I never leave my hometown.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.040
But, even if there were no practical benefits 
to learn a second language,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.300
learning another language simply makes you smarter.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.080
Do you want to be STUPID?

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.640
Learning a second language 
actually makes you brain grow.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.380
- It increases your memory.
- It increases your attention span.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.120
- It helps you deal with ambiguities 
and nuance In meanings

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.860
- It makes you more at multitasking 
and adapting to new circumstances.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.300
And learning a second language helps
offset the beginning of dementia.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.060
By years up to five years in fact.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.240
(Wow)

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.380
Learning a language is brain exercise.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.820
Learning a language also helps to in less tangible ways

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.140
It helps you understand different ways of seeing reality.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.620
Look at this color right here, what color this?

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.340
If you're an English speaker, 
you probably thought that's blue.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.540
Now, have a look at this color right here, ...
what color is that?

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.500
if you're an English-speaker, you might be thinking: 
"Well, that's a shade of blue. That's light blue."

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.080
But, if you're Japanese, 
then you probably thought this is a different color.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.860
That's not blue, it's actually "watercolor".

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.120
in Japanese, these are two different colors.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.640
People perceive them as different colors 
because they have two different names for these colors.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.720
But, wait that doesn't make sense,
they're just two shades of the same color.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.260
I don't understand!
Oh really?

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.820
Okay, well how about this color right here?

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.080
Would you say that's red?

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:23.680
Okay.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.200
How about this color right here?

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.120
if you're an English-speaker, 
you probably said that's pink.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.940
but, there are a lot of languages in the world 
that do not see this as pink.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.060
They see this as a shade of red.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:40.220
The same way that you see light blue as a shade of blue.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.560
(Weird)

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.940
These ways of perceiving reality 
are the core of what we call culture.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.800
By understanding different ways of perceiving reality,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.300
we are understanding cultural variation, 
cultural difference.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:59.800
That greater cultural understanding can bring people 
together, can create greater international relationships

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.440
and can maybe even prevent WARS!

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:09.220
How many wars have broken out because 
the two sides couldn't comprehend each other way 
of perceiving the situation?

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.860
I can only imagine.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.060
So, Anglophones, stop being the laughingstock 
of the world and stop selling yourself short.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.000
Start learning a foreign language 
and expand your mind.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.620
(Hmmm...)

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.440
This is Paul. 
Thank you for watching the LangFocus channel.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.440
Leave your comments down below.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:26.960
Talk to you later, have a good night.

